# Gate recipes

Every gate writes its exit code to a file, then gets a receipt under its own check name.
`ppl transition done` looks for two of these check names literally: `focused-tests` and
`secret-scan`. Get those two names wrong and the run blocks at the very end with no
obvious cause - use the names below verbatim, they are not placeholders.

A piped command returns the exit code of the pipe tail, and a red build has been read as
green that way before - that is why every step below writes `$?` to a file immediately,
instead of trusting a scrollback read.

Every path below is spelled out literally, on purpose. The guard hook fails closed on a
redirect whose target it cannot resolve, and a `$VAR/...` or `$(...)` target is exactly
that: an earlier version of this recipe used `RUN=$(mktemp -d)` and `> "$RUN/install.log"`,
and the hook refused the build line for any role that ran it, and for the orchestrator whenever
its session's cwd sat inside the worktree (post-branch review, finding 3). So: one scratch
directory per task, `/tmp/ppl-<TASK-ID>`, written into every line by hand; `<SHA>` read off
`git -C <worktree> rev-parse HEAD` in its own call and pasted in; `<GENERATION>` from
`ppl stage enter` or `ppl recover` (SKILL.md "The lease"). Run each line below as its own
Bash call. `tests/test_guard.py` feeds this whole block, placeholders substituted, through
the hook - a change here that the hook would refuse fails the suite.

None of the calls below pass `--producer`, and that is deliberate, not an omission: when it
is left out, `ppl evidence add` fills it with `{"provider": "claude-code-subagent", "model":
"unknown", "params_hash": ""}`. Pass `--producer '<json object>'` only if you need to record
something more specific than that default - it is a free-form object, not schema-checked
beyond "is a JSON object".

<!-- include: harness/gates -->

```bash
<!-- include: harness/gate-recipes -->

<!-- include: harness/extra-gates -->

# secret scan before any push - this check name is load-bearing, see above
git -C <worktree> diff origin/<!-- include: harness/default-branch -->...HEAD | grep -in -e password -e secret -e token -e 'BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY' \
  > /tmp/ppl-<TASK-ID>/secrets.log 2>&1; test ! -s /tmp/ppl-<TASK-ID>/secrets.log; echo $? > /tmp/ppl-<TASK-ID>/secrets.rc
ppl evidence add --check secret-scan --result <pass|fail from secrets.rc> --sha <SHA> \
  --log /tmp/ppl-<TASK-ID>/secrets.log --rc /tmp/ppl-<TASK-ID>/secrets.rc --generation <GENERATION> \
  --from orchestrator
```

Baseline delta is what matters, not colour. A failure present at the base SHA is
`BASELINE_FAILURE` and belongs to the repository, not to this candidate. A failure that is
new on the candidate is a regression and belongs to rework.

`--result` also accepts `skip`, for a gate that genuinely does not apply to this candidate
(for example, integration tests on a docs-only change). Use it sparingly and only when the
gate could not have caught anything here - `focused-tests` and `secret-scan` are never
skippable in practice, since `ppl transition done` requires a `pass` receipt for those two
by name and treats a `skip` there the same as a missing receipt.

A rework produces a new commit, and a new commit means a new candidate SHA. Every receipt
above is filed under `<SHA>` at the time it ran, so after a rework the entire block runs
again against the new `<SHA>` - there is no way to carry a passing receipt forward from the
previous candidate, by design (see the same-SHA rule in `SKILL.md` Stage 4).

## Why the guard hook denies these git commands outright

- `git add -A` / `git add .` - git add -A can sweep a live credential into a commit, add explicit paths.
- a bare `git stash` - the stash stack is shared across worktrees; use
  `git stash push -u -m <tag>` instead.
- `git stash pop`, `git stash clear` and a bare `git stash drop` - denied separately from the
  bare form, same reason: the stack is shared, so a pop or a bare drop here takes the top
  entry, which is likely another session's, and a clear eats all of them. Use
  `git stash apply <sha>`, then drop your own entry by the index it is re-found at:
  `git stash list` to find your tag, then `git stash drop stash@{N}`.
- `git push --force` / `-f` / a `+` refspec (`+main`, `+HEAD:main`) - irreversible on a
  shared ref.
- `git reset --hard` - discards worker output that has no receipt yet.

These are denied by the `PreToolUse` guard hook for any `Bash` call, everywhere, not scoped
to this run and not just a style preference - do not attempt a workaround (a different flag
spelling, a subshell) to get past the hook.

For a worker role the same hook also runs `git rm`, `git mv`, `git checkout <ref> -- <path>`
and `git restore` through the write boundary like a redirect, and refuses `git apply`,
`git checkout <ref>`, `git merge`/`rebase`/`pull`/`cherry-pick`/`revert`/`clean`, `patch` and
an unresolvable `rsync` outright: those rewrite tree paths it cannot name from the command
line. `git add`, `git commit`, `git status`/`diff`/`log`/`show` and `git stash push`/`apply`
are unaffected.
